Now let me understand the crash. The crash is "Invalid-free" in `frame_get_lazychunk`. Let me look at the `end:` label logic again:

```c
end:
  if (fp != NULL) {
    fclose(fp);
  }
  if (rc < 0) {
    if (needs_free) {
      free(*chunk);
      *chunk = NULL;
    }
    return rc;
  }
```

The bug: `if (needs_free)` checks the pointer (always non-NULL), not the value `*needs_free`. So even when `frame_special_chunk` set `*needs_free = false` and freed the chunk, `frame_get_lazychunk` will still call `free(*chunk)` again. But `frame_special_chunk` sets `*chunk = NULL` after freeing, so `free(NULL)` is a no-op...

Wait, let me re-read `frame_special_chunk`:
```c
if (rc < 0) {
    free(*chunk);
    *needs_free = false;
    *chunk = NULL;
}
```

So `*chunk = NULL` and `*needs_free = false`. Then in `frame_get_lazychunk`:
```c
if (rc < 0) {
    if (needs_free) {  // pointer is non-NULL, always true
      free(*chunk);    // free(NULL) - no-op
      *chunk = NULL;
    }
    return rc;
}
```

That's fine. But what if `frame_special_chunk` succeeds (rc >= 0)? Then `*needs_free = true` and `*chunk` points to a malloc'd buffer. Then `frame_get_lazychunk` returns `lazychunk_cbytes` (which is `BLOSC_EXTENDED_HEADER_LENGTH`). Then in `frame_decompress_chunk`:

